-- #1617/#1618 BYSTANDER — the new machinery is PRESENT and nothing asserted
-- here uses it.
--
-- "The feature works" is what the sibling fixtures test. This one tests the
-- question no gate normally asks: is the code that has NOTHING to do with the
-- change still correct? The change adds a new head-tag bucket (`__fun__`) and
-- makes `headTyNode` peel effect rows, both of which are consulted by EVERY
-- dispatch in the program, not only by the ones that need them. So an
-- arrow-headed impl and an effect-headed impl are declared below and never
-- dispatched to, while the assertions are about:
--
--   * an ordinary ADT-headed impl of the SAME interface (`sz True`),
--   * a plain non-interface function (`unwrap`),
--   * prelude dispatch that predates all of this (`==` at `List Int`).
--
-- Expected `(48, True)`, hand-derived: `unwrap (Box 41)` = 41; `sz True` = 7
-- via `impl Sz Bool` (NOT the arrow impl, which `Bool` cannot match, and NOT
-- the effect impl, whose head peels to `Int`); 41 + 7 = 48; and
-- `[1, 2, 3] == [1, 2, 3]` is `True`. Every one of those values is what the
-- program printed before this change too — that is the point of the fixture.
interface Sz a where
  sz : a -> Int

-- present, never dispatched to
impl Sz (Int -> Int) where
  sz _ = 5

-- present, never dispatched to
impl Sz (<Stdout> Float) where
  sz _ = 6

impl Sz Bool where
  sz _ = 7

data Box a = Box a

unwrap : Box Int -> Int
unwrap (Box n) = n

main = println (unwrap (Box 41) + sz True, [1, 2, 3] == [1, 2, 3])
